# Projected gradient descent

Projected gradient descent is a local optimizer for smooth functions with box
bounds. It takes a step along the negative gradient, then clamps each parameter
to its permitted interval. This gives a simple way to keep iterates feasible
without storing a Hessian approximation.

[API
reference](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/projected_gradient_descent/struct.ProjectedGradientDescent.html)

  | Property    | Requirement                                             |
  | ----------- | ------------------------------------------------------- |
  | Objective   | A smooth scalar function                                |
  | Evaluations | `CostFunction` and `Gradient`                           |
  | Search      | Local, from one starting point                          |
  | Constraints | A box-only `ConstraintSet<BoxBounds<V>>` on the problem |
  | Parameters  | All four dense backends, with `f32` or `f64`            |

## When to use it

Consider projected gradient descent when each parameter has an independent lower
or upper bound and you want a method with little working storage. It is also a
useful baseline for comparing more elaborate bound-constrained solvers. Gradient
projection methods are discussed in [Nocedal and Wright
(2006)](#ref-nocedal-wright-2006).

For a smooth problem where curvature information could reduce the number of
iterations, compare [L-BFGS-B](../../#lbfgsb). For an unconstrained problem, use
[gradient descent](../gradient-descent/) or
[L-BFGS](../../quasi-newton/l-bfgs/). Linear and nonlinear constraints need a
method that supports those blocks, such as [SLSQP](../../#slsqp).

The method follows local descent directions. Different starting points can lead
to different constrained minima, and a poorly scaled objective can make progress
slow even though every published iterate satisfies the box bounds.

## How it works

Basin first projects the starting point into the box, then evaluates the
objective and gradient there. At each iteration it chooses a step length, moves
along the negative gradient, and projects the result back into the box.
Projection clamps each coordinate to its lower and upper bounds. Equal bounds
pin a coordinate at a fixed value.

At a constrained minimum, the negative gradient can point out of the box. The
relevant stationarity test measures the infinity norm of
`x - projection(x - gradient)`. This residual vanishes when a unit gradient step
followed by projection returns to the same point. It accounts for active bounds,
so it can vanish even when the raw gradient is nonzero.

`ProjectedGradientDescent::new(alpha)` uses a fixed step length. Basin also
accepts a custom line search, but that search may evaluate unconstrained trial
points before projection. Its decrease conditions do not automatically carry
over to the projected point.

## Example

This quadratic has its unconstrained minimum at `[1.0, -2.0]`. The box moves the
constrained minimum to `[0.5, -1.0]`, where the raw gradient is nonzero. The
example declares bounds on the problem, uses a fixed step, and stops on the
projected-gradient residual.

<!-- compile: solver-projected-gradient-descent -->
```rust
use basin::{
    BoxBounds, ConstraintDescription, ConstraintSet, Constraints, CostFunction,
    Executor, Gradient, ProjectedGradientDescent, Termination,
};
use std::convert::Infallible;

struct BoundedQuadratic {
    lower: Vec<f64>,
    upper: Vec<f64>,
}

impl Constraints for BoundedQuadratic {
    type Set = ConstraintSet<BoxBounds<Vec<f64>>>;

    fn constraints(
        &self,
    ) -> <Self::Set as ConstraintDescription>::Borrowed<'_> {
        ConstraintSet::new()
            .with_bounds(BoxBounds::new(Some(&self.lower), Some(&self.upper)))
    }
}

impl CostFunction for BoundedQuadratic {
    type Param = Vec<f64>;
    type Output = f64;
    type Error = Infallible;

    fn cost(&self, x: &Self::Param) -> Result<f64, Self::Error> {
        Ok((x[0] - 1.0).powi(2) + 4.0 * (x[1] + 2.0).powi(2))
    }
}

impl Gradient for BoundedQuadratic {
    type Gradient = Vec<f64>;

    fn gradient(&self, x: &Self::Param) -> Result<Self::Gradient, Self::Error> {
        Ok(vec![2.0 * (x[0] - 1.0), 8.0 * (x[1] + 2.0)])
    }
}

fn main() {
    let problem = BoundedQuadratic {
        lower: vec![-1.0, -1.0],
        upper: vec![0.5, 1.0],
    };
    let solver = ProjectedGradientDescent::new(0.1)
        .with_absolute_projected_gradient_tolerance(1e-8);

    let result = Executor::from_start(problem, solver, vec![-3.0, 5.0])
        .max_iter(200)
        .run()
        .unwrap();

    assert!(matches!(
        result.report.termination,
        Termination::Converged(_)
    ));
    assert!((result.param()[0] - 0.5).abs() < 1e-7);
    assert!((result.param()[1] + 1.0).abs() < 1e-7);
}
```

The starting point lies outside the box. Initialization projects it to
`[-1.0, 1.0]` before the first objective or gradient evaluation.

## Practical considerations

### Choose a step for your objective's scale

A small fixed step can make progress slow, while an overly large one can cause
oscillation or repeated jumps between bounds. Rescale parameters and check
objective values as you tune the step. The value `0.1` is appropriate for the
example's curvature; it is not a general default.

### Use a projected stationarity test

Configure `with_absolute_projected_gradient_tolerance` for the infinity norm of
`x - projection(x - gradient)`. The check is disabled by default; zero tests
exact stationarity. Choose the tolerance for your parameter units and derivative
accuracy. Optional step and cost-change checks can also stop a run, but a small
observed change alone does not establish stationarity.

### Keep budgets on the executor

Set iteration and evaluation limits there, then inspect the termination report.
Reaching a budget and satisfying a convergence test give different reasons for
stopping.

### Check where the objective is defined

The fixed-step strategy evaluates the projected iterates. A custom line search
may call your objective and gradient outside the box. If those evaluations are
invalid, use a fixed step or a solver with a bound-aware search, such as
L-BFGS-B.

## Implementation

Bounds belong to the problem through `Constraints`, using a box-only
`ConstraintSet<BoxBounds<V>>`. The solver requires this declaration and rejects
unsupported constraint blocks. It validates the bounds and projects the seed
before publishing the first evaluated point through `FirstOrderState`.

Each accepted iteration publishes the projected point with its matching
objective and gradient. The solver stores the step strategy; it does not keep
curvature history or momentum. Fresh initialization resets the line search,
while exact solver-and-state checkpoints retain its history.

Supported parameter types are `Vec<F>`, `nalgebra::DVector<F>`,
`ndarray::Array1<F>`, and `faer::Col<F>`, with `F = f32` or `f64`. The method
uses vector operations and coordinate-wise clamping without matrix
factorizations or a BLAS/LAPACK provider. Custom line searches and optional
convergence checks can require additional vector capabilities. See the API's
[Backends
section](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/projected_gradient_descent/struct.ProjectedGradientDescent.html#backends)
for the requirements.

